## Why This Is Asked
This interview question tests dynamic programming with prefix-state tracking and solution reconstruction. The inner minimum calculation forces careful leftward scanning, providing a tangible beginner-to-intermediate challenge while remaining implementable in O(n²k) time.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over prefixes with block count tracking
- On-the-fly maintenance of minimum values over sliding windows
- Solution reconstruction via backpointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k), Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
      let currentMin = Infinity;
      for (let p = i; p >= t; p--) {
        currentMin = Math.min(currentMin, A[p-1]);
        const candidate = (dp[p-1][t-1] + currentMin * (i-p+1));
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          pre[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = pre[i][t];
    blocks.unshift([p, i]);
    i = p - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a non-trivial partition DP with a substring cost that requires precomputation and reconstruction, combining both combinatorial choices and an aggregate cost function.

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitions with a cost function over substrings
- Efficient precomputation of substring distinct counts
- Backtracking reconstruction of cut points
- Time/space trade-offs and memory optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: precompute cost[p][i], then DP with backpointers
const n = s.length;
const INF = 1e9;
const cost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(0));
for (let p = 0; p < n; p++) {
  const seen = new Array(26).fill(false);
  let distinct = 0;
  for (let i = p; i < n; i++) {
    const idx = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
    if (!seen[idx]) { seen[idx] = true; distinct++; }
    cost[p][i] = distinct;
  }
}
const kCap = Math.min(k, n);
const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(kCap+1).fill(INF));
const prv = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(kCap+1).fill(-1));
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, kCap); t++) {
    for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
      const cand = dp[p][t-1] + cost[p][i-1];
      if (cand < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; prv[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a non-trivial DP with a non-additive cost function and reconstruction, plus a precomputation layer for inversions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix length i and blocks t with transition from a previous breakpoint j
- invCost(l,r) as block cost (inversions within subarray)
- Reconstruction pointers for block boundaries
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk) or O(nk) with rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function precomputeInv(a){
  const n=a.length; const inv=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let cnt=0;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      for(let i=l;i<r;i++) if(a[i]>a[r]) cnt++;
      inv[l][r]=cnt;
    }
  }
  return inv;
}
```

